config KERNEL_SHELL_LUA_MODULE
	tristate "Kernel Shell Lua Scripting Module (Demo)"
	depends on KERNEL_SHELL_LUA
	depends on MODULES
	help
	  This option enables the Lua scripting module for the kernel shell.
	  The module provides Lua scripting capabilities that can be used
	  during panic conditions for advanced debugging and recovery.

	  To compile this driver as a module, choose M here: the module will be
	  called kernel_shell_lua.

	  If unsure, say N.

config KERNEL_SHELL_LUA_REAL_MODULE
	tristate "Kernel Shell Real Lua Scripting Module"
	depends on KERNEL_SHELL_LUA
	depends on MODULES
	select LUA_LIB
	help
	  This option enables the real Lua scripting module for the kernel shell.
	  The module integrates actual Lua 5.5.0 interpreter for full Lua
	  scripting capabilities during panic conditions.

	  This provides complete Lua language support including:
	  - Full Lua interpreter with standard libraries
	  - Kernel-specific API functions
	  - Real script execution with proper error handling
	  - Memory management for kernel space

	  To compile this driver as a module, choose M here: the module will be
	  called kernel_shell_lua_real.

	  This requires the Lua source code to be available.

	  If unsure, say N.

config KERNEL_SHELL_NODEJS_MODULE
	tristate "Kernel Shell Node.js Scripting Module (Demo)"
	depends on KERNEL_SHELL_NODEJS
	depends on MODULES
	help
	  This option enables the Node.js scripting module for the kernel shell.
	  The module provides JavaScript scripting capabilities that can be used
	  during panic conditions for advanced debugging and recovery.

	  To compile this driver as a module, choose M here: the module will be
	  called kernel_shell_nodejs.

	  If unsure, say N.

config KERNEL_SHELL_NODEJS_REAL_MODULE
	tristate "Kernel Shell Real Node.js Scripting Module (QuickJS)"
	depends on KERNEL_SHELL_NODEJS
	depends on MODULES
	select QUICKJS
	help
	  This option enables the real Node.js scripting module for the kernel shell.
	  The module integrates QuickJS JavaScript engine for full JavaScript/Node.js
	  scripting capabilities during panic conditions.

	  This provides complete JavaScript language support including:
	  - ES2020 compliant JavaScript engine
	  - Node.js-like console object
	  - Kernel-specific API functions
	  - Real script execution with proper error handling
	  - Memory management for kernel space

	  To compile this driver as a module, choose M here: the module will be
	  called kernel_shell_nodejs_real.

	  This requires the QuickJS source code to be available.

	  If unsure, say N.

config LUA_LIB
	bool
	help
	  This option is selected by modules that need Lua library support.

config QUICKJS
	bool
	help
	  This option is selected by modules that need QuickJS JavaScript engine.

config EXAMPLE_SCRIPTABLE_MODULE
	tristate "Example Scriptable Module"
	depends on KERNEL_SHELL_SCRIPTING
	depends on MODULES
	help
	  This option builds an example module that demonstrates how to make
	  kernel modules scriptable using the kernel shell scripting interface.

	  The module provides:
	  - Custom scripting commands (increment, decrement, set_message, get_info)
	  - Standard scripting operations (init, exit, suspend, resume, config, status, debug)
	  - Proc file interface for monitoring
	  - Example of Lua and JavaScript integration

	  This is primarily for demonstration and testing purposes.

	  To compile this driver as a module, choose M here: the module will be
	  called example_scriptable_module.

	  If unsure, say N.

config KERNEL_SHELL_TEST_MODULE
	tristate "Kernel Shell Test Module"
	depends on KERNEL_SHELL
	depends on MODULES
	help
	  This option builds a test module for the kernel shell that provides
	  testing commands and utilities for verifying shell functionality.

	  The module provides:
	  - test_input: Test input handling in kernel shell
	  - test_kbd: Test direct keyboard input access
	  - test_panic: Trigger a test panic to activate kernel shell

	  This is useful for testing and debugging the kernel shell functionality,
	  especially the new graphical PTY interface and keyboard input handling.

	  To compile this driver as a module, choose M here: the module will be
	  called kernel_shell_test.

	  If unsure, say N.
